## ⚠️ Architectural Finding: retrieval-core Type Mismatch

> [!CAUTION]
> **retrieval-core is product-centric and does not fit the travel domain.**
>
> The current contracts in retrieval-core:
> - `Product` has only `product_id` and `title` — no coordinates, opening hours, or city
> - `CorpusAdapter` protocol has `get_product(product_id)` / `mget_products(product_ids)` — returning `Product`
> - `ScoredHit` references `product_id`, not a generic `doc_id`
>
> TripAgent needs a `Venue` with `venue_id`, `name`, `city`, `coord`, `source_id`, `source_version`, and adapters that return venue-like documents.
>
> **Per AGENTS.md and prompt #8, I must NOT work around this with a local type.** This is the exact mismatch the prompt said to report.
>
> **Options (your call):**
> 1. **Generalize retrieval-core** before Day 3 — rename `Product` → `Document` (with a generic metadata dict), rename `product_id` → `doc_id` in `ScoredHit`, rename `CorpusAdapter.get_product` → `get_document`. ShopRank would then subclass/alias `Document` as `Product`. This is the cleanest long-term fix.
> 2. **Add a parallel `Document` model** to retrieval-core alongside `Product` — less disruptive to ShopRank but messier.
> 3. **Defer retrieval-core integration** — build TripAgent's `Venue` locally today, and reconcile when retrieval-core is generalized. This explicitly violates prompt #8 but avoids blocking.

